## Proof

We prove the identity via the Fourier sine transform and its inversion.

**Fourier sine transform pair.** For a function $f$ on $[0,\infty)$ that is piecewise smooth and absolutely integrable, the sine transform and its inversion are
$$F(\omega) = \int_0^\infty f(x)\sin\omega x\,dx, \qquad f(x) = \frac{2}{\pi}\int_0^\infty F(\omega)\sin\omega x\,d\omega,$$
with the inversion holding pointwise at every point of continuity of $f$.

**Define the target function.** Let
$$f(x) = \begin{cases} \sin x, & 0 \le x < \pi, \\ 0, & x > \pi. \end{cases}$$
This $f$ is bounded, compactly supported, and piecewise $C^1$, hence $f\in L^1\cap L^2$, so the inversion formula applies at all continuity points (i.e. all $x\ne \pi$).

**Compute the sine transform.**
$$F(\omega) = \int_0^\pi \sin x\,\sin\omega x\,dx = \frac12\int_0^\pi\bigl[\cos((1-\omega)x)-\cos((1+\omega)x)\bigr]dx.$$
Integrating,
$$F(\omega) = \frac12\left[\frac{\sin((1-\omega)x)}{1-\omega}-\frac{\sin((1+\omega)x)}{1+\omega}\right]_0^\pi = \frac12\left[\frac{\sin((1-\omega)\pi)}{1-\omega}-\frac{\sin((1+\omega)\pi)}{1+\omega}\right].$$
Using $\sin((1-\omega)\pi)=\sin(\pi-\omega\pi)=\sin\pi\omega$ and $\sin((1+\omega)\pi)=\sin(\pi+\omega\pi)=-\sin\pi\omega$,
$$F(\omega) = \frac{\sin\pi\omega}{2}\left[\frac{1}{1-\omega}+\frac{1}{1+\omega}\right] = \frac{\sin\pi\omega}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{1-\omega^2} = \frac{\sin\pi\omega}{1-\omega^2}.$$

**Convergence note.** At $\omega=1$ the apparent singularity is removable: $\sin\pi\omega = -\pi(\omega-1)+O((\omega-1)^3)$ and $1-\omega^2=-(\omega-1)(1+\omega)$, so the ratio $\to \pi/2$. At infinity the integrand is $O(1/\omega^2)$ (after expanding $\sin\pi\omega\sin\omega x$ as a sum of cosines), so the integral converges absolutely for every fixed $x$.

**Apply inversion.** By the inversion formula, at every continuity point of $f$,
$$\frac{2}{\pi}\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin\pi\omega}{1-\omega^2}\sin\omega x\,d\omega = f(x).$$

Since $f$ is even-extended as an odd function ($\sin x\cdot\mathbf 1_{|x|<\pi}$ is odd), the same formula holds for negative $x$ by odd symmetry of both sides. Therefore
$$\frac{2}{\pi}\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin\pi\omega}{1-\omega^2}\sin\omega x\,d\omega = \begin{cases} \sin x, & |x|<\pi,\\ 0, & |x|>\pi. \end{cases}$$

(At $|x|=\pi$ the inversion yields the midpoint $\tfrac12(\sin\pi+0)=0$, consistent with $\sin\pi=0$.) $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
